    "result_text": "All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`). `AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class; `AcceptanceBase` is abstract and `AuditLogCapture` is a helper.\n\n## Summary\n\nI consolidated the three separate duplicate checks (telephone, email, household) into a single derived `identityKey` and reject with 409 only on a whole-key match.\n\n**Key insight from the test:** owners `a` and `b` share a telephone but have different names/addresses and no email, yet must collide (409). That only holds if `householdId` is empty for both \u2014 i.e. `householdId` must *not* be auto-derived per owner. So `householdId` is now assigned only when the request opts into a shared household and a matching owner already exists (matching the OpenAPI doc), making it empty for ordinary owners. The key is `normalizedTelephone|email|householdId`.\n\n**Changes (kept small and cohesive to limit structural impact):**\n\n1. **New `OwnerIdentityPolicy`** (self-contained, low internal complexity) \u2014 derives `identityKey`, conditionally assigns `householdId`, and rejects on an exact full-key match via `DuplicateIdentityException` (`@ResponseStatus(CONFLICT)`).\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 replaced the three policy calls with one `OwnerIdentityPolicy.rejectDuplicateIdentity(...)` call (and pruned now-unused imports), keeping the edit inside the large controller minimal.\n3. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 one `@Mapping` line so the derived `identityKey` is returned, matching how other derived fields are exposed.\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `identityKey` response property.\n5. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 one small handler mapping the new exception to 409 (required, since unmapped runtime exceptions fall through to 500).\n\n**Behavioural change, as specified:** an email-only or household-only overlap with a *different* telephone is now allowed; two members of the same household with different telephones both succeed; only an exact full-`identityKey` match is a duplicate. The old telephone/email/household policy classes remain untouched (unused) rather than deleted, to avoid touching extra files.",
